      ":question/problem": "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/4}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-2}}\nthe integral is convergent.\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-3}}\nthe integral is divergent.\n\nEvaluating our integral in the convergent case, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{{e}^{t/4}}=\\frac{4}{4s-1},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-5}}."
    },
